  "query_preview": "/Sunday through Thursday/...\nA series of low pressure systems from the eastern Pacific will \nmove into the western US through the extended period. The first\nwill approach the coast on Sunday, then split with the bulk of \nthe energy slowly progressing inland over the Southwest Monday \nand Tuesday. Dry and breezy conditions will continue on Sunday \nahead of this system with southwesterly flow increasing \ntemperatures several degrees from Saturday. The trough will hold\ntogether enough for a 50-70% chance of light precipitation late\nSunday through Tuesday. Snow levels will fall from 5500-6500 \nfeet Sunday night to 4000-6000 feet by Tuesday, lowest across \neastern Oregon. Temperatures will cool a few degrees each day \nwith lighter winds. After brief dry conditions, another low \nwill enter the Pacific Northwest from the Gulf of Alaska mid to \nlate week. This low may contain colder air than previous \nsystems, but confidence in the low progression and extent of \ncold air is low due to model variability. With the uncertainty, \nprecipitation chances rise to 30-50% late Wednesday through \nThursday, with temperatures favored to cool a few more degrees \nto near normal and snow levels around 3500-5000 feet.",
